1.31.0: curves by wavelength

'polajuice curves' gains two panels and a second table, for reading a
look as a physicist would:

- spectral sensitivity of the rendered brightness against wavelength,
  with the eye's V(lambda) for reference. Computed from the look's
  response to each primary around a middle gray: S = g . M cmf(lambda).
  A red filter on a B&W camera moves it toward red and to nothing in
  the blue; the identity is the eye's own curve.
- by wavelength, the color: white with monochromatic light mixed in at
  an excitation purity of 0.20 (what sRGB holds at every wavelength),
  followed through the look - dominant wavelength shift in nm and
  purity out over in, for 445-645 nm, where hue has a wavelength.
- --table prints both per wavelength.
- stated on the chart and in the docs: an RGB pipeline holds three
  numbers of a spectrum, so these describe the look's treatment of a
  camera's rendering of each wavelength, not a film's measured curves.
  CIE 1931 observer by the analytic fit of Wyman, Sloan, Shirley (2013).
- release.sh keeps charts and styles written into the repo's top folder
  out of the way (/*.svg, /*.dtstyle ignored).

Tests: compat (identity = the eye, no hue shift, purity 1; red filter
weighs red up and blue-green down; the panels are in the chart).
